sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch04-04> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The visible polarity of a cell (symmetry of structure about an axis, such as nucleus–centrosome or free–attached surface) is the outward expression of a real polarised distribution of forces, whose lines of force are made visible where particles of matter become strung together under those forces.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Morphological polarity of the cell is the outward sign of a dynamical polarity";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter IV, “On the Internal Form and Structure of the Cell”; topic “Polarity of the resting cell”. Scope: cells with recognisable axes of symmetry, e.g. ova, columnar and ciliated epithelial cells"
}
